solution_steps:
We can use the Euclidean Algorithm to simplify the expression $\frac{a^3+b^3}{a+b}$.
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}\left(\frac{a^3+b^3}{a+b}, ab\right) &= \text{gcd}\left(\frac{(a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2)}{a+b}, ab\right) \\
&= \text{gcd}(a^2-ab+b^2, ab).
\end{align*}
Since $a-b=6$, we have $a=b+6$. Substituting into the expression, we get
\begin{align*}
a^2-ab+b^2 &= (b+6)^2 - (b+6)b + b^2 \\
&= b^2 + 12b + 36 - b^2 - 6b + b^2 \\
&= 6b + 36 \\
&= 6(b+6).
\end{align*}
So the expression becomes $\text{gcd}(6(b+6), ab)$. Since $a-b=6$, we have $ab = (b+6)b = b^2 + 6b$. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(6(b+6), ab) &= \text{gcd}(6(b+6), b^2 + 6b) \\
&= \text{gcd}(6(b+6), 6b) \\
&= 6\text{gcd}(b+6, b).
\end{align*}
Since $\text{gcd}\left(\frac{a^3+b^3}{a+b}, ab\right) = 9$, we know that $6\text{gcd}(b+6, b) = 9$. The only possible value for $\text{gcd}(b+6, b)$ is 3. Therefore, $b+6$ and $b$ are both multiples of 3, and $b$ must be a multiple of 3. The smallest possible value of $b$ is $\boxed{3}$.
The answer is: 3

