solution_steps:
We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 91 and 72.
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(91, 72) &= \text{gcd}(72, 91 - 72) \\
&= \text{gcd}(72, 19).
\end{align*}
Then,
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(72, 19) &= \text{gcd}(19, 72 - 3 \cdot 19) \\
&= \text{gcd}(19, 15).
\end{align*}
Finally,
\begin{align*}
\text{gcd}(19, 15) &= \text{gcd}(15, 19 - 15) \\
&= \text{gcd}(15, 4) \\
&= \text{gcd}(4, 15 - 3 \cdot 4) \\
&= \text{gcd}(4, 3).
\end{align*}
Since we have reached $\text{gcd}(4, 3)$ and 4 does not divide evenly into 3, we know that the GCD is 1.
Therefore, the greatest common divisor of 91 and 72 is $\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1

To determine if the decimal representation of a fraction terminates, we need to look at the prime factorization of the denominator.
