solution_steps:
Let $A = \frac{\sin 18^\circ \cos 12^\circ + \cos 162^\circ \cos 102^\circ}{\sin 22^\circ \cos 8^\circ + \cos 158^\circ \cos 98^\circ}$.
Using the identities $\sin (180 - \theta) = \sin \theta$ and $\cos (180 - \theta) = -\cos \theta$, we can rewrite the numerator and denominator as follows:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\sin 18^\circ \cos 12^\circ + \cos 162^\circ \cos 102^\circ}{\sin 22^\circ \cos 8^\circ + \cos 158^\circ \cos 98^\circ} &= \frac{\sin 18^\circ \cos 12^\circ - \cos 18^\circ \cos 78^\circ}{\sin 22^\circ \cos 8^\circ - \cos 22^\circ \cos 82^\circ} \\
&= \frac{\cos 12^\circ (\sin 18^\circ - \cos 78^\circ)}{\cos 8^\circ (\sin 22^\circ - \cos 82^\circ)}.
\end{align*}
Using the sum-to-product formula $\sin A - \sin B = 2 \cos\left(\frac{A+B}{2}\right) \sin\left(\frac{A-B}{2}\right)$, we can further simplify the numerator and denominator:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\cos 12^\circ (\sin 18^\circ - \cos 78^\circ)}{\cos 8^\circ (\sin 22^\circ - \cos 82^\circ)} &= \frac{\cos 12^\circ (2 \cos 48^\circ \sin 30^\circ)}{\cos 8^\circ (2 \cos 52^\circ \sin 30^\circ)} \\
&= \frac{\cos 12^\circ \cos 48^\circ}{\cos 8^\circ \cos 52^\circ}.
\end{align*}
Using the double angle formula $\cos 2\theta = 2 \cos^2 \theta - 1$, we can rewrite the numerator and denominator as follows:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\cos 12^\circ \cos 48^\circ}{\cos 8^\circ \cos 52^\circ} &= \frac{\frac{1}{2} \left( \cos 60^\circ + \cos 36^\circ \right)}{\frac{1}{2} \left( \cos 60^\circ + \cos 44^\circ \right)} \\
&= \frac{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \cos 36^\circ}{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \cos 44^\circ}.
\end{align*}
Using the half angle formula $\cos \frac{\theta}{2} = \pm \sqrt{\frac{1 + \cos \theta}{2}}$, we can evaluate $\cos 36^\circ$ and $\cos 44^\circ$:
\begin{align*}
\cos 36^\circ &= \sqrt{\frac{1 + \cos 72^\circ}{2}} = \sqrt{\frac{1 + \frac{1}{2}}{2}} = \frac{\sqrt{5}}{4}, \\
\cos 44^\circ &= \sqrt{\frac{1 + \cos 88^\circ}{2}} = \sqrt{\frac{1 + \frac{1}{2}}{2}} = \frac{\sqrt{5}}{4}.
\end{align*}
Substituting these values back into the expression, we get:
\begin{align*}
\frac{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \cos 36^\circ}{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \cos 44^\circ} &= \frac{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{\sqrt{5}}{4} \right)}{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{\sqrt{5}}{4} \right)} \\
&= \frac{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{5}}{8}}{\frac{1}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{5}}{8}} \\
&= \frac{1 + \frac{\sqrt{5}}{4}}{1 + \frac{\sqrt{5}}{4}} \\
&= \boxed{1}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: 1

