solution_steps:
We can first compute $\mathbf{A}^2$ and $2 \mathbf{A}$ as follows:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^2 &= \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 1(1) + 3(2) & 1(3) + 3(1) \\ 2(1) + 1(2) & 2(3) + 1(1) \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 7 & 6 \\ 4 & 7 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
\begin{align*}
2 \mathbf{A} &= 2 \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 3 \\ 2 & 1 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 2(1) & 2(3) \\ 2(2) & 2(1) \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 6 \\ 4 & 2 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Then $\mathbf{A}^2 - 2 \mathbf{A}$ is given by subtracting the corresponding entries:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^2 - 2 \mathbf{A} &= \begin{pmatrix} 7 & 6 \\ 4 & 7 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 6 \\ 4 & 2 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 7 - 2 & 6 - 6 \\ 4 - 4 & 7 - 2 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} 5 & 0 \\ 0 & 5 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Finally, we compute the determinant of $\mathbf{A}^2 - 2 \mathbf{A}$:
\begin{align*}
\det (\mathbf{A}^2 - 2 \mathbf{A}) &= \det \begin{pmatrix} 5 & 0 \\ 0 & 5 \end{pmatrix} \\
&= 5(5) - 0(0) \\
&= \boxed{25}.
\end{align*}The answer is: 25

